    <entry>
        <title>Cheese Agent: A FastAPI RAG Chatbot with Vector Search</title>
        <author>
            <name>Mariia Osipova</name>
        </author>
        <link href="https://mariia-osipova.github.io/my-page/cheese-agent/"/>
        <id>https://mariia-osipova.github.io/my-page/cheese-agent/</id>
        <media:content url="/my-page/media/posts/5/Group-42-3.png" medium="image" />
            <category term="jeje"/>
            <category term="jaja"/>

        <updated>2025-01-20T18:25:02-03:00</updated>
            <summary type="html">
                <![CDATA[
                        <img src="/my-page/media/posts/5/Group-42-3.png" alt="" />
                    AI-powered cheese agent built with FastAPI, AstraDB vector search, OpenAI, and a lightweight JS frontend.
                ]]>
            </summary>
        <content type="html">
            <![CDATA[
                    <p><img src="/my-page/media/posts/5/Group-42-3.png" class="type:primaryImage" alt="" /></p>
                <p>This post is about my experience building <a href="https://github.com/mariia-osipova/cheese-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cheese Agent</a>, a cheese-themed RAG chatbot project with a FastAPI backend, OpenAI embeddings, AstraDB vector search, and a vanilla JS frontend.</p><h2>What this post is</h2><p>This is a short technical reflection on building Cheese Agent, especially the parts that made it feel like a real systems project rather than just a UI experiment.</p><h2>Why I made it</h2><p>I wanted to build something that combined an interface people would actually enjoy using with a backend architecture that forced me to think about retrieval quality, data organization, and deployment.</p><h2>How it works</h2><p>The backend exposes a chat endpoint in FastAPI. When a user sends a question, the backend embeds that query with OpenAI, searches AstraDB, and sends the retrieved context into the generation step.</p><h2>What I focused on in the implementation</h2><p>The most useful parts for me were the ingestion script, the vector database, and the split between a simple frontend and a backend that handled retrieval and answer generation.</p><h2>What seems promising so far</h2><p>This kind of project is useful because it combines data preparation, embeddings, retrieval, API design, frontend integration, and deployment in one place.</p><h2>Closing thoughts</h2><p>For me, Cheese Agent became a compact full-stack AI project where the model, the database, the data pipeline, and the interface all had to line up.</p>
            ]]>
        </content>
